WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.269
You'll now set up the second agent for your system,

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.838
the file suggestion agent.

3
00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.941
This agent will help you choose the relevant files

4
00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:10.944
from your structured data to build the domain graph.

5
00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:15.248
Let's go. In the workflow that you're working through,

6
00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:18.151
you're building the Structured Data Agent workflow.

7
00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:21.154
In Lesson 4, you created the User Intent Agent.

8
00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:24.557
Here in Lesson 5, you will create the File Suggestion Agent.

9
00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:27.060
It will have a similar pattern to what we did

10
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.162
with the User Intent Agent in Lesson 4.

11
00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.598
The agent itself has one primary goal,

12
00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:33.967
creating this approved files list.

13
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.869
This will be the list of files

14
00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.871
that the agent itself has looked at,

15
00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:41.374
decided these look relevant for
what the user's trying to do,

16
00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.577
and then ask the user, hey, do you approve these?

17
00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:47.580
And if so, the output will be the approved_files.

18
00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.883
To do that, the File Suggestion Agent

19
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.651
has a bunch of tools to actually take

20
00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.453
a look at the files that are available.

21
00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:55.188
So that's the list_import_files.

22
00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:58.158
For any of those files, it can sample_file.

23
00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:00.460
That's like doing a little bit of a read from the file.

24
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.262
It can only read about 100 lines.

25
00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.564
And then based on that, it can make a recommendation

26
00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.967
based using this tool called
the set_suggested_files tool.

27
00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.170
And then if the user approves,

28
00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.338
it'll call the approved
suggested files tool,

29
00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:14.507
and that will update the memory so

30
00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:16.743
that we have the approved files in context

31
00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.745
rather than just the suggested files.

32
00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.014
All of this of course informed

33
00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:23.049
by the work of the previous agent.

34
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.552
All this is based on the overall direction

35
00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:27.654
of what the user is trying to accomplish.

36
00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:32.058
This agent gets that from memory rather
than from conversational history.

37
00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:34.828
So rather than relying on the transcript of what's

38
00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:38.131
happened before this tool was called
or before this agent was called.

39
00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:41.501
This agent's going to use the get_approved_user_goal tool

40
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.737
to find out what it is that the user's trying to accomplish.

41
00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:46.739
Then with that, take a look at the files,

42
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.508
figure out which ones are relevant

43
00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:50.310
and make a suggestion to the user.

44
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.512
Let's take a look at the code.

45
00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:55.215
You'll start with the usual setup,
importing the libraries that we need

46
00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.917
and any helper functions that we defined previously.

47
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.288
With the libraries all ready, we'll go ahead and create

48
00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:04.691
a connection to OpenAI and make sure that it's working.

49
00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.028
Oh, it's very consistent today. Yes,

50
00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:10.897
I'm ready. How can I assist you?

51
00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:15.535
You'll start by constructing
the instructions for the agent itself.

52
00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.705
Now we're going to do this a little bit at a time.

53
00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:22.609
First you want to define the agent's role and also its goal.

54
00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.245
So for this agent that is in charge

55
00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:27.413
of making suggestions for files that are relevant,

56
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.616
we're going to say that it's a

57
00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:31.518
constructive critic reviewing a list of files.

58
00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:34.821
And its goal is to suggest relevant files

59
00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:37.624
that can be used for constructing a knowledge graph.

60
00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.360
You can then give it some hints

61
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.028
about how to go about achieving that task.

62
00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:45.398
The particular task of course is to review the list of files

63
00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:48.601
and see from within those files, which ones are relevant for

64
00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.803
the kind of graph and the description

65
00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.406
that is based on the approved user goal.

66
00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:56.643
For each of those files, there's a tool available called

67
00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.845
sample file that will let the agent

68
00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.047
actually see the content of that file

69
00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:03.750
to confirm whether the file contains information

70
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.652
that's useful for achieving the goal.

71
00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.353
And we're giving it also the hint

72
00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.855
that it should only take a look at

73
00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:11.457
structured data files like CSV or JSON.

74
00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:15.261
You can close that out with
the chain of thought directions.

75
00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:17.964
And here the chain of thought really has two pieces.

76
00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:20.266
There's going to be a preparation part of it,

77
00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:23.269
which is go ahead and call the get_approved_user_goal

78
00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.972
to get what the user goal is from session memory.

79
00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.141
We're going to call the tool for that

80
00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:30.176
rather than passing that in directly to the prompt.

81
00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.078
So encourage the agent to use the tool.

82
00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.247
And then with that in hand, we're

83
00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:36.282
going to give it the chain of thought.

84
00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:39.686
As always, think carefully, repeating
these steps until you're finished.

85
00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:42.288
And here are the steps we want the agent to follow.

86
00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:44.591
Get a list of all the files that are available.

87
00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.926
Then for that list of files,

88
00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.529
evaluate each one of them for their relevance,

89
00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:52.532
and then use the set_suggested_files tool

90
00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.034
to record the suggested files into memory.

91
00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:57.837
This step here, step 3,

92
00:03:57.837 --> 00:04:00.540
encouraging the agent to use
the get_suggested_files tool

93
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.343
to get the list of
suggested files is really quite important.

94
00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:07.146
It's possible for the agent
to realize within its own reasoning

95
00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:09.148
and within conversational history

96
00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:11.117
what the list of files might be

97
00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:13.152
and what it thinks the suggested files are.

98
00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:16.456
rather than relying on that,
we really want to encourage it

99
00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:18.558
to only use the files that have

100
00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:20.660
been saved to the suggested files memory.

101
00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.664
Here the get_suggested_files tool is what accesses that memory,

102
00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:27.867
completing that loop actually encourages
the LLM to really focus on

103
00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:31.537
what is in memory,
not what it remembers from conversational history.

104
00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:34.207
With that then, the result that you got back

105
00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:36.676
from the tool call is what's going to be presented

106
00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.478
to the user for approval.

107
00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:40.179
If the user approves,

108
00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.181
then go ahead and say that's great.

109
00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.150
User approved suggested files.

110
00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.153
But if the user has feedback, go back to step 1

111
00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.756
and repeat the process with the feedback in mind.

112
00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:54.494
You can now define the tools that are used by the agent.

113
00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:57.997
The first tool is
get_approved_user_goal.

114
00:04:58.398 --> 00:05:00.566
This function is defined
in the tools module

115
00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:01.934
that is provided to you.

116
00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:04.270
It just returns the
approved user goal

117
00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.039
that has been
stored in the state.

118
00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.574
We're also going to import a
load helper function here

119
00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:11.978
that these tools that
we're about to define will use.

120
00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:14.580
Each of these tools
interact with the file system.

121
00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.149
But it's not the
entire file system,

122
00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:17.917
they're constrained
to just looking at

123
00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:19.652
the files that are
available for import

124
00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:22.155
that Neo4j itself can access.

125
00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.524
So Neo4j has the notion
of an import directory,

126
00:05:24.791 --> 00:05:26.359
so this helper is going to
let us know

127
00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:27.927
where is that import directory,

128
00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:30.296
all the tools then can
use that import directory

129
00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:32.398
for deciding what files are available,

130
00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.534
and of those files,
which ones to use.

131
00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.271
You can then define the tool to list the import files,

132
00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.074
and it's going to be saved to
this key ALL_AVAILABLE_FILES.

133
00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:43.576
The tool call needs the tool_context

134
00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.345
because there are some helper utilities we're

135
00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:47.347
going to use for actually getting the directory

136
00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.749
on disk where these files are stored,

137
00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:52.051
and so that the files that are listed

138
00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:54.754
are only relative paths rather than absolute paths.

139
00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:57.256
So you can see down here in the implementation,

140
00:05:57.256 --> 00:06:00.159
we're going to get this helper function, get_neo4j_import_dir.

141
00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.663
that's the import directory that Neo4J itself has access to.

142
00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:06.466
Neo4J does not have access to the full disk space,

143
00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:08.067
so we can't use absolute files.

144
00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:11.170
It has to be relative to this import directory. So we get

145
00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.639
here. And then we list all of the files.

146
00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:17.143
This is just some string using the import directory.

147
00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:18.444
We then go ahead and do

148
00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:19.946
a glob listing of all the files

149
00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:22.348
recursively down from that directory

150
00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:24.650
and do a bit of list comprehension here

151
00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.652
to turn that into a relative list of files.

152
00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.023
We save that to a current state as ALL_AVAILABLE_FILES

153
00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:33.626
and then return that as the result of this tool call.

154
00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.630
You can then define the sample_file tool call.

155
00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:41.901
Now, the sample_file tool is again going
to use the tool_context,

156
00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:44.504
and it's also going to use a file_path.

157
00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:47.206
This is going to be a relative path to the file

158
00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.709
that the LLM has decided it wants to take a look at.

159
00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:53.112
This tool could be instead a call out

160
00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:55.815
to the operating system
to actually use cat or something.

161
00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:57.817
Here we're going to do this all in Python.

162
00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:00.520
The result of calling this tool is going to be

163
00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.121
just up to 100 lines of text.

164
00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:03.589
We're going to assume it's a

165
00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:05.324
text file. Get 100 lines of text,

166
00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:07.527
return that as the value of this tool call.

167
00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.763
Now we have a couple of guards in place here

168
00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:12.865
because the LLM can invent paths,

169
00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:14.667
it can invent lots of different things.

170
00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:16.869
When it calls this, we're going to do some sanity checking

171
00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:18.638
on the file_path that was passed in.

172
00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:21.374
We're going to make sure that it's not absolute,

173
00:07:21.374 --> 00:07:23.876
so that if it is absolute, we're going to return an error

174
00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:26.312
because it must be only relative files and we

175
00:07:26.312 --> 00:07:28.748
expect them to be relative to the import directory.

176
00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:32.151
And the last part here, we also encourage
the agent to remember

177
00:07:32.151 --> 00:07:34.954
that the file path being passed in here

178
00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:37.757
should appear in the list of available files.

179
00:07:38.157 --> 00:07:39.959
So if it's not sure, could actually

180
00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:42.028
check that. And based on the import directory,

181
00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:43.696
we go ahead and get the full path to

182
00:07:43.696 --> 00:07:45.331
that file. We make sure that the file exists.

183
00:07:45.331 --> 00:07:47.333
Again, return a helpful error message

184
00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:50.736
if it doesn't exist so that the agent can take
corrective measures.

185
00:07:51.237 --> 00:07:52.672
And if it is existing, then

186
00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:54.340
we go ahead and read 100 lines

187
00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.342
and save that into the content,

188
00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:58.744
passing that back as a successful tool call.

189
00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:01.113
If any errors occur here,

190
00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:02.648
we're going to pass that error back

191
00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.417
as a response to the LLM as well.

192
00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:05.985
And the agent can decide whether

193
00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:07.820
or not it's capable of doing anything

194
00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:09.222
or it just needs to complain to

195
00:08:09.222 --> 00:08:10.623
the user that something is just wrong.

196
00:08:10.923 --> 00:08:13.226
You've given the agent the ability to list all the files,

197
00:08:13.226 --> 00:08:14.927
take a look at those files. So

198
00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:16.829
now it's able to actually make a suggestion

199
00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:18.731
based on those files and the file content

200
00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:20.633
about what it thinks are relevant files for

201
00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:22.335
what the user is trying to get done.

202
00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:25.238
Here we've got a pair of tools that actually does that.

203
00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:27.740
The first is to set the list of suggested files.

204
00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.843
It takes a list of strings that should be file paths.

205
00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:32.979
And you can add some extra checking here

206
00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:34.747
if you wanted to to make sure that

207
00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:37.750
the list passed in here are all actually existing files.

208
00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.619
Here we're actually going to be a bit

209
00:08:39.619 --> 00:08:41.754
trustful, a bit of YOLO in this tool here.

210
00:08:41.754 --> 00:08:44.457
Of course, for an improvement,
you can add that check.

211
00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:46.459
And then the flip side, of course,

212
00:08:46.459 --> 00:08:48.728
we're going to have a get_suggested_files

213
00:08:48.728 --> 00:08:50.229
that just looks into memory for that

214
00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:51.731
list of files that have been suggested.

215
00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:57.470
Once the agent has successfully figured out what files

216
00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.138
they think are relevant for the user's

217
00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:01.040
goal and made that suggestion to the user.

218
00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.109
If the user has approved, of

219
00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:05.244
course, it will call the approve_suggested_files tool

220
00:09:05.344 --> 00:09:08.748
to make that transfer from the state of suggested files

221
00:09:08.748 --> 00:09:11.951
into approved files. As in the previous lesson,

222
00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:15.154
we're going to double check that before this call is made

223
00:09:15.454 --> 00:09:18.157
that the SUGGESTED_FILES actually exist in state,

224
00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:20.660
and if not, suggest to the agent that

225
00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.128
it needs to go and figure

226
00:09:22.128 --> 00:09:23.763
that out before it calls this tool.

227
00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:25.097
If it has been set, it

228
00:09:25.097 --> 00:09:26.332
will go ahead and make a copy

229
00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:28.234
of the SUGGESTED_FILES into the APPROVED_FILES

230
00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:31.137
and return that as the success from this tool call.

231
00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:33.472
You can then go ahead and take all

232
00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:35.641
of those tool definitions, put those into a list,

233
00:09:35.641 --> 00:09:38.544
and save that as the file_suggestion_agent_tools.

234
00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:41.147
We'll use that for the agent definition in just a moment.

235
00:09:41.547 --> 00:09:43.349
You can now define your agent.

236
00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:45.851
The agent definition is pretty straightforward.

237
00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:47.453
We did all the hard work in setting

238
00:09:47.453 --> 00:09:49.055
up the tools and defining the prompt earlier.

239
00:09:49.055 --> 00:09:51.657
We're just going to pass those in here and create the agent.

240
00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:55.161
With the agent in hand, you can

241
00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:56.929
now go ahead and interact with the agent.

242
00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.433
Import the make_agent_caller helper that we defined earlier,

243
00:10:00.733 --> 00:10:02.835
and then create a caller using that.

244
00:10:03.202 --> 00:10:06.205
Alright, just really just one thing
that's a little bit different here.

245
00:10:06.706 --> 00:10:09.508
Previously when we used the make_agent_caller,
we would just call it

246
00:10:09.508 --> 00:10:12.011
directly with the agent we want to execute.

247
00:10:12.511 --> 00:10:14.714
Notice here that we're initializing the state

248
00:10:15.014 --> 00:10:17.016
with the approved_user_goal.

249
00:10:17.416 --> 00:10:19.418
Now, as we had said at the beginning of the workflow,

250
00:10:19.719 --> 00:10:22.021
the approved_user_goal is just the overall direction

251
00:10:22.021 --> 00:10:24.423
for all the different agents
and what they're trying to accomplish.

